  /* The flares: a quarter circle cut out of the tab's color on each side, its edge in the same hairline. Every tab has
     them, folded into its bottom corners; the open tab's unfold as its color comes in, so a tab taken or tapped turns
     from the plain box into the open tab's shape in one move, and the one left folds back the same way. */
  /* Each reaches a pixel into the tab: at 125% or 150% zoom its inner edge would be half painted, and the tab's side
     hairline would show through it down to the bottom, closing the curve into a little triangle.
     Three layers. The curve grows out of the tab's corner (its size is what moves). Under it, a column of the tab's
     color two pixels wide grows with it over the tab's edge, where at some zooms the curve's last pixel is left
     unpainted; the column starts about √r down, below where the curve's hairline leaves the edge, so the side hairline
     still runs into the curve. A pixel of the tab's color along the bottom is always there: it covers the strip's
     bottom line beside the tab just as the tab does under itself, so while a tab opens or closes that line fades evenly
     under it, corners included, instead of reaching in to the edge until the curve gets there. It stops 3px short of
     the curve's far end, where the line runs on into the curve. */
  .tab::before,
  .tab::after {
    --cut: calc(1.75px + 0.14 * var(--r));
